`registry export --as toml` renders a slice back to registry TOML through
`zenkey::slice_to_toml`, the inverse of `parse_slice` — so what comes out can
be fed back in (#50). Offline here: `--registry` reads the checkout's own
files, and no bus is opened.

The per-producer `[budget]` table (RFC 08 §2, v1.32) rides the slice like
every other carried field and comes back out where the TOML grammar needs a
plain table to be — right after the header block, before the first
`[[subject]]`. A slice that declares none emits none.

```console
$ zenctl registry export --registry ../fixture-tests/registry --producer sysinfo --as toml
# ── sysinfo.toml ─────────────────────────────
[registry]
version = "1.1"
app = "zensight"
convention = 1

[producer]
name = "sysinfo"
description = "host CPU/memory/disk/net metrics"

[budget]
rss_mb = 32

[[subject]]
...

```
